# Lapis Cloud -- ELB SECOND INSTANCE coturn config. This is a TEMPLATE, not the real file: the
# placeholders below are substituted from deploy/production-elb/.env by render-secrets.sh (see
# ../production/README.adoc "Video conferencing"). The rendered turnserver.conf is gitignored --
# never commit it.
#
# `realm` differs from ../production/'s copy, as does `min-port`/`max-port` -- found live
# 2026-08-25 ("could not establish pc connection"): coturn allocates a TURN relay address from its
# OWN configured min-port/max-port range and hands that address back to the browser literally, the
# same "port number baked into the protocol payload" issue livekit.yaml.template's own comment
# describes. A relay allocated from 51000-51019 (PdV's own externally-published range) would be
# unreachable here, since ELB only publishes 51020-51039 externally -- so min-port/max-port below
# must equal the externally-published range, not just docker-compose.yml's host-side mapping.
# `listening-port` itself stays 3478 (safe to remap on the host side only, see
# docker-compose.yml's own comment) -- only the relay range needed this treatment.
listening-port=3478
min-port=51020
max-port=51039
realm=elb.parteidervernunft.de
use-auth-secret
static-auth-secret=${LAPIS_TURN_SECRET}
external-ip=${LAPIS_PUBLIC_IP}
no-tls
no-dtls
fingerprint
log-file=stdout
no-cli

# ── TURNS over TLS (port 443) -- PREPARED, DELIBERATELY INACTIVE ────────────────────────────────
# Why: a browser with a restrictive WebRTC privacy policy (Brave's Shields / fingerprinting
# protection) or a network that only permits outbound 443 can block BOTH the direct ICE path AND
# plain TURN on 3479/UDP+TCP (this instance's host-side remap, see docker-compose.yml). Symptom:
# audio/video never start even though the camera/microphone permission was granted. Reported live
# by an ELB board member, 2026-09-10. See ../production/README.adoc, "TURNS over TLS (port 443)".
#
# NOT enabled yet, and enabling it is NOT a code change: it needs (a) a DNS A record
# (turn.elb.parteidervernunft.de -> this host's IP -- a DIFFERENT name than the pdv2 instance's,
# see README.adoc "DNS"), (b) a real certificate for that name, and (c) Caddy SNI-based TCP
# multiplexing on 443, because Caddy already owns port 443 on this host. coturn itself therefore
# listens on an INTERNAL port, never on 443 directly.
#
# `tls-listening-port` stays 5350 CONTAINER-side, same value as ../production/'s copy -- the
# host-side publish differs (5351 here), same remap scheme `listening-port` 3478->3479 already
# uses above, because both instances share one host.
#
# To activate, in this order: remove `no-tls` above, uncomment the three lines below, point
# cert-file/pkey-file at the real certificate, then re-run render-secrets.sh and restart coturn.
#tls-listening-port=5350
#cert-file=/etc/coturn/certs/turn-fullchain.pem
#pkey-file=/etc/coturn/certs/turn-privkey.pem
